{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the scope-index census, child f51f16f4, at exact head b5deace0148c11bceec3da416945b9f55ad9595e (tree 3af22242), under builder promise d28d9bcd and ruling c0789349. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, on one security point. A non-operator caller can reach the census's expensive proof work before any rate limit applies.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- Base and delta. The head descends from Main 7b220547 (c7b7d1b8 only adds the sprint report). It changes 25 paths, matching the 25 artifacts. Gate checkpoint 0d9663a5 has tree fab3764d, as stated. After it, only the note and the manifest change.\n- Evidence. The manifest SHA-256 is ce302d79…, as stated. All 132 files match it, nothing is writable, and SHA256SUMS has 131 lines, all OK.\n- Gate. gate-followup/receipt.json records exit 0 at 0d9663a5, tree fab3764d, with source unchanged and no collection errors. The failed first gate, the failed Native runs and the inconclusive row-bound baseline are kept separately.\n- Controls. Removing the install-signer guard turns the real member's Signed census from 403 into 200. Removing the row bound saves an oversized manifest. Both are retained.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe authority chain matches c0789349:\n- the pin comes from `artifactsRegisterScope`;\n- the index row for the pin must have kind `register` and a genesis digest;\n- a bounded exact `proofSource` read of seq 0 must give an applied genesis whose seed hashes to the pin, with a supported `platform:register@` definition, a matching incarnation, an `install` founding with `to: null` and a verified signature whose digest is the seed cause, and an actor equal to the request's signer.\n\nThe request's expiry, the pin and the row are checked again after the proof wait.\n\nThe census is one new predicate beside `checkSignedRead`, not routed through it. Adding `census` to `SIGNED_READS` grants no ordinary read, because `checkSignedRead` refuses a read name that differs from the route's (signed-reads.ts:324).\n\nThe mode is evaluated only after authorization, so a non-operator learns nothing about it. The page is exactly `{rows,next}`. `next` is null only when no row follows: one `LIMIT 1` probe, so a full final page needs no extra request. Both the owner and the route check that rows are ordered and that `next` equals the last row.\n\nThe CLI matches the ruling:\n- it pages at most ceil(4096/100)+1 = 42 times;\n- it bounds bytes, chunks and time;\n- it refuses a redirect;\n- it writes the file only for a complete, nonempty manifest that contains the configured root, in the unchanged v1 form;\n- it prints one outcome line, with a nonzero exit unless the census is complete;\n- its qualification states the window, the legacy gap and that operator-key succession is out of scope.\n\nThe ScopeIndexPort gains only `list`, and has/register are unchanged.\n\n## Security: change requested\n\nEvery ordinary signed read passes the edge admission `admitServing(env, { kind: \"signed-read\", reader }, address)` before any object work. It runs through `readGate` in `api()` (worker.ts:219-225), and query-items calls it directly (worker.ts:443). That admission enforces the address window and the key window.\n\nThe census route skips it. `routedRequest` sends `/v1/scope-index/census` to `scopeIndexCensusRoute(request, env)` without the caller's address. The route then calls the fixed owner after checking only the request's form and signature (`requestOf`).\n\nKeys are free to make, so anyone can sign a well-formed census request for the pinned register. For each such request, the singleton `applied-genesis-v1` owner does three things:\n- it reads the register row;\n- it makes a proof RPC to the register object, bounded at 10 s and 1 MiB;\n- it waits up to 10 s.\n\nOnly then does it refuse with `forbidden`. The owner's own limiter runs after operator authorization and with `address: null`, so it bounds only the operator's own key. That singleton owner also handles serving admission and creation admission for the whole Worker. The ruling counted on \"the key read rate limit … bounds it\", but as wired, nothing bounds non-operator callers.\n\nPlease do the following:\n1. Pass `address` into the census route.\n2. Run the same edge `admitServing(env, { kind: \"signed-read\", reader }, address)` after `requestOf` and before `owner.list`, mapping `invalid` to forbidden as `readGate` does.\n3. Then remove the owner's inner `rate` call, or keep exactly one key accounting, so the operator is not charged twice.\n4. Add a witness: a non-operator key that exceeds the read windows is refused `rate-limited` before any proof RPC.\n5. Update the route rows in docs/security/entry-points.md: \"key limit before listing\" becomes \"address and key windows before the owner\".\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe source is compact for what it checks: 127 lines for the owner and route, and 133 for the CLI. A possible later reduction, not requested here: the owner could keep the verified install signer for the pinned row in memory, keyed by the row, instead of fetching the proof on every page. This head deliberately avoids new state, and that choice is fine.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: changes requested, for the missing admission before the owner described above.\n- Simplification: approved.\n\nBeyond the source and inventory edits, the recut owes the census tests and a fresh gate, as docs/testing.md requires for a source change.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6c289901edc00417aacc58af1b4baf0fedcca0da","binding":"assigned","head":"b5deace0148c11bceec3da416945b9f55ad9595e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d2561792ce31646df5d702e4dbfc0ed5644e3122\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d28d9bcd02aa9374774c52e565f5b5c893c657f1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d2561792ce31646df5d702e4dbfc0ed5644e3122","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}